<DocsLayout
  activeSlug="dogs"
>
  <article>
    <h1>Dogs</h1>
    <ReferencePills slug="dogs" />
    <p class="lede">
      A dog is a process the shepherd runs for its own sake, not for yours.
      It watches the flock instead of being part of it. Two ship inside the{" "}
      <code>shep</code> binary: <code>metrics</code>, a Prometheus endpoint,
      and <code>bark</code>, webhook alerts. You can also point{" "}
      <code>shep adopt</code> at a binary of your own and the shepherd will
      supervise it the same way.
    </p>

    <ShortVersion>
      <CodeBlock>{`$ shep enable metrics`}</CodeBlock>
    </ShortVersion>

    <Callout variant="note">
      A dog is not the same thing as a sheep with a shepherd channel: see{" "}
      <a href="/docs/shepherd-channel">the shepherd channel</a> for that.
      A sheep opens fd 3 and talks newline JSON to the daemon that already
      owns it. A dog is a separate client: it connects to{" "}
      <code>shep.sock</code> and speaks the same wire protocol{" "}
      <code>shep flock</code> or <code>shep describe</code> would.
    </Callout>

    <h2 id="what-a-dog-actually-is">What a dog actually is</h2>
    <p>
      Underneath, a dog is not a second kind of supervised process. It's an
      ordinary sheep with a marker on its entry saying where it came from
      (built-in, or adopted). The kill ladder, the backoff curve, the
      restart budget, what <code>Errored</code> means: none of it branches
      on whether an entry is a dog. A dog that restarts is still a dog when
      it comes back, and a dog whose binary won't spawn shows up in{" "}
      <code>shep dogs</code> as <code>Errored</code>, the same way a broken
      sheep would.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>shep flock</code> prints the flock as two tables (sheep, then
      dogs) and <code>shep dogs</code> prints the second one alone. Both
      built-ins always appear, <code>disabled</code> until enabled. A
      wildcard selector (<code>stop all</code>, <code>reload all</code>,{" "}
      <code>/regex/</code>) never touches a dog; naming one exactly always
      does, so <code>shep disable bark</code> reaches a dog that a sweep
      leaves running.
    </p>

    <h2 id="turning-one-on">Turning one on</h2>
    <VerbSignature verb="enable" usage="shep enable <name>">
      <p>
        <code>enable</code> and <code>disable</code> also take a sheep's
        name, which is{" "}
        <a href="/docs/lifecycle#what-stays-what-goes-the-muster-roll">
          a different verb
        </a>
        . What follows is the dog half.
      </p>
      <p>
        Writes <code>shep.toml</code> first, then starts the dog now if a
        shepherd is listening. Run it with nothing listening and it still
        exits 0: the config says the dog belongs, and the next shepherd
        that boots brings it up. Nothing here autostarts a shepherd on your
        behalf. Two verbs do, and neither is a dog verb:{" "}
        <code>shep start</code> and <code>shep muster</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Takes a built-in name or one <code>shep adopt</code> has recorded.
        Any other name exits 3 and lists the dogs, or 5 with no shepherd.
      </p>
    </VerbSignature>
    <VerbSignature verb="disable" usage="shep disable <name>">
      <p>
        The mirror: stops the dog if one is running and removes it from the
        boot list either way. <code>shep flock</code> keeps listing it,{" "}
        <code>disabled</code>. It carries no name check of its own,
        deliberately, so it is what takes a hand-written{" "}
        <code>enabled_dogs</code> entry back out.
      </p>
    </VerbSignature>
    <VerbSignature verb="delete" usage="shep delete <name>">
      <p>
        The one way to remove a dog: it is stopped, deregistered, and struck
        from <code>enabled_dogs</code>, <code>adopted_dogs</code> and{" "}
        <code>channel_dogs</code> in <code>shep.toml</code>, so the next boot
        does not bring it back. Its <code>&lt;name&gt;</code> section in{" "}
        <code>dogs.toml</code> stays, webhook URLs and all, and the verb
        says so: those settings are yours rather than the adoption's. A
        built-in dog is refused by the shepherd, and the refusal names{" "}
        <code>shep disable</code> as the verb that exists for one.
      </p>
    </VerbSignature>
    <CodeBlock>{`$ shep enable metrics
NAME     SOURCE    SHEPHERD  STATUS
metrics  built-in  true      online

$ shep dogs
ID  NAME     STATUS    PID    RESTARTS  EXIT  CPU  MEM    UPTIME  SOURCE    ACCESS
0   metrics  online    49313  0         -     -    10.9M  2s      built-in  -
1   bark     disabled  -      0         -     -    -      0s      built-in  -

$ shep flock
ID  NAME  STATUS  PID    RESTARTS  EXIT  CFG  CPU  MEM   UPTIME  FOLD  SMIT
2   web   online  49290  0         -     -    -    3.0M  2s      -     -

Dogs
ID  NAME     STATUS    PID    RESTARTS  EXIT  CPU  MEM    UPTIME  SOURCE    ACCESS
0   metrics  online    49313  0         -     -    10.9M  2s      built-in  -
1   bark     disabled  -      0         -     -    -      0s      built-in  -

$ shep disable metrics`}</CodeBlock>
    <p class="fine">
      The guard is on the verb, not on the file. Write a name into{" "}
      <code>[daemon] enabled_dogs</code> by hand and the shepherd still
      reads it at boot, still treats anything outside{" "}
      <code>[daemon] adopted_dogs</code> as built-in, and spawns{" "}
      <code>shep dog &lt;name&gt;</code> for it once per restart until the
      budget is spent. From <code>shep dogs</code> that reads as a climbing
      restart count against a <code>SOURCE</code> of <code>built-in</code>,
      for a name that is not one; <code>shep bleats &lt;name&gt;</code> is
      where the refusal itself shows up.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      A dog's name is not a sheep's to take, built-in or adopted. Starting a
      new sheep named <code>metrics</code> is refused, whether the dog runs
      or sits disabled. A sheep that already holds the name keeps it: the
      shepherd skips the dog, warns at boot to rename one of the two, and{" "}
      <code>shep enable metrics</code> says a sheep is already registered
      under it.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      The dogs table shares its column order with the sheep table above it,
      so a dog and a sheep read the same way. <code>SOURCE</code> is the one
      column only dogs have, and <code>CFG</code>, <code>FOLD</code> and{" "}
      <code>SMIT</code> the three only sheep have. See <a href="/docs/output#the-dogs-table">terminal
      output</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      A dog whose <code>STATUS</code> reads <code>silent</code> is running
      but has never spoken to the shepherd, usually because a{" "}
      <code>cargo install</code> replaced its binary and the old process is
      still up. <code>shep bleats &lt;dog&gt;</code> has the refusal;{" "}
      <code>shep restart &lt;dog&gt;</code> is the fix. See <a
      href="/docs/output#a-dog-that-never-answers">terminal output</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      Running <code>enable</code> and <code>adopt</code> at once from a
      provisioning script is safe: each takes an exclusive lock on{" "}
      <code>shep.toml</code> for its whole read-edit-write, so the second
      waits its turn. <code>dogs.toml</code> has its own lock on the same
      terms, held by both things that write it: the migration a boot runs
      once, and the config pane's own edits. A boot holding both takes{" "}
      <code>shep.toml</code>'s first.
    </p>

    <h2 id="when-a-dog-starts">When a dog starts</h2>
    <p>
      After the flock, by default. A metrics dog that came up first would
      spend the boot answering for a flock that is not running yet.
    </p>
    <p>
      A log-rotation dog is the other way round, since it has to be running
      before a sheep starts writing. Put its name in{" "}
      <code>[daemon] boot_first_dogs</code> and it runs ahead of every
      sheep:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="$SHEP_HOME/shep.toml">{`[daemon]
enabled_dogs    = ["metrics", "log-rotate"]
boot_first_dogs = ["log-rotate"]`}</CodeBlock>
    <p class="fine">
      A name here that is not in <code>enabled_dogs</code> is inert:
      promoting a dog does not enable it. This is <code>shep.toml</code>{" "}
      rather than <code>dogs.toml</code> for the same reason{" "}
      <code>adopted_dogs</code> is: the <code>[&lt;name&gt;]</code> table in{" "}
      <code>dogs.toml</code> is passed through to the dog itself, and a
      shep-owned key inside it would collide with a third-party dog's own
      schema. See <a href="/docs/boot-order">boot order</a> for the stages
      this positions a dog in.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      <code>[daemon] channel_dogs</code> lives there too, written by{" "}
      <code>shep adopt</code> for a dog that <a
      href="/docs/writing-a-dog#asking-for-the-channel">asks for the shepherd
      channel</a>. Such a dog takes <code>shep trigger</code>.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      <code>shep daemon reload</code> carries every dog holding a process,
      marked or not, and leaves a parked one behind: a dog that is disabled
      and stopped is registered at rest by the successor the way a cold boot
      registers it, under a new id. A dog <code>enabled_dogs</code> dropped is
      stopped and marked, running or already stopped, so it reads{" "}
      <code>disabled</code> and <code>shep enable &lt;name&gt;</code> is the
      way back.
    </p>

    <h2 id="configuration-lives-on-the-socket-not-the-environment">Configuration lives on the socket, not the environment</h2>
    <p>
      A dog's settings live under <code>[&lt;name&gt;]</code> in{" "}
      <code>$SHEP_HOME/dogs.toml</code>, hand-editable and separate from{" "}
      <code>shep.toml</code>, and they never travel through the dog's
      environment. Instead the dog connects to the socket and asks for its
      own section over the wire. The daemon reads the file fresh for every
      such request rather than serving a cached copy.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      <code>shep adopt</code> fills that section in when the dog's manifest
      carries <code>defaults</code> and the section is not there yet; it
      never rewrites one that is.
    </p>
    <p>
      An environment variable is readable from the process table, inherited
      by every child the dog spawns, and captured into a crash dump.{" "}
      <code>[bark.sinks]</code> routinely holds a webhook URL, and a
      webhook URL is a bearer credential. Keeping the whole section off the
      environment means nobody has to remember which dog's config is
      sensitive.
    </p>
    <Callout variant="careful">
      Editing <code>[&lt;name&gt;]</code> in <code>dogs.toml</code> does not
      reach a dog that's already running. It asked for its section once, at
      startup, and nothing pushes it a new one. <code>shep disable
      &lt;name&gt; &amp;&amp; shep enable &lt;name&gt;</code> is what
      re-reads it: a fresh process asking a fresh question.
    </Callout>

    <h2 id="three-places-a-dogs-config-can-live">Three places a dog's config can live</h2>
    <p>
      <code>[&lt;name&gt;]</code> in <code>dogs.toml</code>, above, is the
      dog's own settings. A sheep's Flockfile entry can also carry{" "}
      <code>[app.dogs.&lt;name&gt;]</code>, one table per dog for that sheep
      alone: shep stores it and hands it to the dog it names, reading
      nothing inside. See <a href="/docs/writing-a-dog#reading-a-sheep-s-own-table">reading
      a sheep's own table</a> for how a dog gets it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>shep copy web.dogs.kelpie api.dogs.kelpie</code> moves one
      sheep's table for a dog onto another sheep, merging into what that
      sheep already holds unless <code>--replace</code> says otherwise.{" "}
      <code>shep get kelpie</code> prints the dog's own section, and{" "}
      <code>shep set kelpie.worker.timeout 60</code> changes one key inside
      it, and <code>shep unset</code> removes one.
    </p>
    <p>
      A dog declaring <code>validate</code> is asked before any write to its
      settings lands, and its refusal stops the write in its own words. A copy
      of a table is one of those writes:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock>{`$ shep set api.dogs.kelpie.git.checkout /srv/other --yes
KEY       CHANGE   OLD       NEW
checkout  changed  /srv/api  /srv/other
error[invalid_config]: nothing was written: \`kelpie\` refused \`api\`'s table for \`kelpie\`: this checkout belongs to another project`}</CodeBlock>
    <p>
      A dog that declared nothing is never asked, and one that isn't running has
      nothing to ask. <a href="/docs/writing-a-dog#being-asked-about-its-settings">Writing
      a dog</a> has what it is asked.
    </p>
    <p>
      A third place, <code>[dog.&lt;name&gt;]</code> at the top level of a
      Flockfile, is read and discarded: shep parses it so the file still
      loads, but keeps nothing from it. It exists for a dog that reads its
      own Flockfile directly.
    </p>

    <details>
      <summary>Live updates without a restart</summary>
    <p>
      A dog can subscribe instead of waiting to be bounced. Shep publishes
      on <code>config.dog.&lt;name&gt;</code> when it changes a dog's
      section itself, and a dog holding a subscription re-asks for its
      section and decides what the change means: swap the values, rebind a
      listener, or ask for its own restart. The frame says the section
      changed, not what is in it. Bark subscribes and swaps in place: sinks
      and rules are pure data. Four things publish today: the boot that moves a section out
      of <code>shep.toml</code>, a write from{" "}
      <a href="/docs/lookout">the dog config pane</a>, which is why an edit
      made there reaches a running bark without bouncing it, a provider
      dog's push that changes a value the section names with{" "}
      <code>{"{{secret:namespace/KEY}}"}</code>, and a change to{" "}
      <code>secrets.json</code> that moves a key the section names with{" "}
      <code>{"{{secret:KEY}}"}</code>. Writing the same value again
      publishes nothing. A hand edit of <code>dogs.toml</code> still needs
      the stop and start above.
    </p>
    <Callout variant="careful">
      A dog that restarts itself on a config change has to say so in its own
      log. Nothing else can tell that apart from a crash loop, and the
      restart count is the column an operator reads as instability.
    </Callout>
    <Callout variant="careful">
      A section that used to live in <code>shep.toml</code> migrates to{" "}
      <code>dogs.toml</code> once, on the first boot of a shep carrying this
      move, and it prints which dogs moved. Any boot does it, including{" "}
      <code>shep runtime</code> and <code>shep dev</code>, and <code>adopt</code>{" "}
      of a dog whose manifest carries <code>defaults</code> migrates first. If
      a name carries values in both files, the daemon refuses to boot until one
      section is deleted, <code>shep daemon reload</code> refuses
      before it signals the shepherd it was going to replace, and so does such
      an <code>adopt</code>. An empty{" "}
      <code>[dog.&lt;name&gt;]</code> is not a value and refuses nothing. A
      dog present in only one file migrates or starts normally.
    </Callout>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>What a provider dog pushes</summary>
    <p>
      A dog can also push, not only be configured. A provider dog sends{" "}
      <code>Request::PutSecrets</code> with its own registered name as the
      namespace, replacing whatever that namespace held for one environment
      rather than merging into it. Pushed values are cached to{" "}
      <code>$SHEP_HOME/secrets-cache.json</code> by default, so a reboot
      does not leave every sheep that reads them waiting on the dog and a
      network round trip; <code>persist = false</code> in the dog's own
      section of <code>dogs.toml</code> turns that off. See{" "}
      <a href="/docs/secrets">Secrets</a> for the token that reads a pushed
      value, and for what a namespace does and does not defend against.
    </p>
    <Callout variant="careful">
      A namespace can legally contain a dot, and an unquoted section header
      does not mean what it looks like. <code>[vercel.prod]</code> in{" "}
      <code>dogs.toml</code> parses as a nested TOML table, key{" "}
      <code>prod</code> inside table <code>vercel</code>, not as one
      literal section named <code>vercel.prod</code>. A namespace with a
      dot in it needs a quoted header, <code>["vercel.prod"]</code>, or the
      section never matches and <code>persist</code> silently falls back to
      its default of <code>true</code>. This is ordinary TOML, and the same
      trap catches any dog name with a dot in it, not only a provider's
      namespace.
    </Callout>
    </details>

    <h2 id="the-metrics-dog">The metrics dog</h2>
    <p>
      <code>shep enable metrics</code> serves Prometheus exposition text over
      plain HTTP, bound to <code>127.0.0.1:9615</code> by default:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="dogs.toml">{`[metrics]
bind = "127.0.0.1:9615"`}</CodeBlock>
    <p>
      The flock and host series are rebuilt per scrape, from a fresh flock
      listing and a fresh host sample. The channel samples are not: the dog
      keeps the last sample per sender, name and label set until that sender
      stops or restarts. Verified by scraping it directly:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock>{`$ curl 127.0.0.1:9615/metrics
shep_sheep_memory_bytes{sheep="web",id="0",fold=""} 3178496
shep_sheep_restart_total{sheep="web",id="0",fold=""} 0
shep_sheep_status{sheep="web",id="0",fold="",status="online"} 1
shep_dog_up{dog="metrics",source="built-in"} 1
shep_daemon_up{version="0.1.0"} 1
shep_daemon_pid 39898
shep_host_memory_total_bytes 51539607552`}</CodeBlock>
    <details>
      <summary>Full metric reference</summary>
    <div class="metrics-table">
      <div class="row header">
        <span>Metric</span>
        <span>Meaning</span>
      </div>
      <div class="row"><span><code>shep_sheep_cpu_percent</code></span> <span data-label="Meaning">Tree CPU as a percentage of one core, over the last sampling window.</span></div>
      <div class="row"><span><code>shep_sheep_memory_bytes</code></span> <span data-label="Meaning">Tree resident set size in bytes.</span></div>
      <div class="row"><span><code>shep_sheep_restart_total</code></span> <span data-label="Meaning">Restart count since registration.</span></div>
      <div class="row"><span><code>shep_sheep_uptime_seconds</code></span> <span data-label="Meaning">Seconds since this sheep's last successful start.</span></div>
      <div class="row"><span><code>shep_sheep_status</code></span> <span data-label="Meaning">1 for the status this sheep is currently in, 0 for every other status.</span></div>
      <div class="row"><span><code>shep_dog_up</code></span> <span data-label="Meaning">1 when this dog is online, 0 otherwise. A disabled dog has no series.</span></div>
      <div class="row"><span><code>shep_daemon_up</code></span> <span data-label="Meaning">Always 1: the scrape reached the shepherd.</span></div>
      <div class="row"><span><code>shep_daemon_pid</code></span> <span data-label="Meaning">The shepherd's own pid, so a restart is visible as a step change.</span></div>
      <div class="row"><span><code>shep_host_memory_total_bytes</code></span> <span data-label="Meaning">Total physical memory on the host.</span></div>
      <div class="row"><span><code>shep_host_memory_used_bytes</code></span> <span data-label="Meaning">Memory in use on the host, as the platform reports it.</span></div>
      <div class="row"><span><code>shep_host_processes</code></span> <span data-label="Meaning">Number of processes running on the host, the flock included.</span></div>
      <div class="row"><span><code>shep_host_uptime_seconds</code></span> <span data-label="Meaning">Seconds since the host booted.</span></div>
      <div class="row"><span><code>shep_channel_samples_refused_total</code></span> <span data-label="Meaning">Channel samples this dog refused, by reason. Absent until the first refusal.</span></div>
    </div>
    </details>
    <p class="fine">
      A reference Grafana dashboard built against this exact metric set ships
      in <code>assets/grafana/</code>. The default bind is loopback on
      purpose. Every series above carries a sheep's name as a label, and on
      plenty of hosts that's the name of an internal service. Widening{" "}
      <code>bind</code> to <code>0.0.0.0</code> is one config line away, but
      metrics won't make that call for you by shipping wide and asking you
      to lock it down.
    </p>

    <h3 id="channel-samples">Channel samples</h3>
    <p>
      A sheep or a dog can send its own numbers on its shepherd channel. The
      dog keeps them and appends them to every scrape, after the groups above
      and beside every export, under the labels shep already uses for that
      process: <code>sheep</code>, <code>id</code> and <code>fold</code> for a
      sheep, <code>dog</code> and <code>source</code> for a dog. <a
      href="/docs/shepherd-channel">The shepherd channel</a> says what to write
      on fd 3.
    </p>
    <div class="metrics-table">
      <div class="row header"><span>Rule</span><span>Refused as</span></div>
      <div class="row"><span>A name matches <code>[a-zA-Z_:][a-zA-Z0-9_:]*</code>, at most 128 bytes</span> <span data-label="Refused as"><code>invalid_name</code></span></div>
      <div class="row"><span>No name starts with <code>shep_</code></span> <span data-label="Refused as"><code>reserved_name</code></span></div>
      <div class="row"><span>A label name matches <code>[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*</code>, at most 64 bytes, no <code>__</code> prefix</span> <span data-label="Refused as"><code>invalid_label</code></span></div>
      <div class="row"><span>No label named <code>sheep</code>, <code>id</code>, <code>fold</code>, <code>dog</code> or <code>source</code></span> <span data-label="Refused as"><code>reserved_label</code></span></div>
      <div class="row"><span>At most 16 labels, 256-byte label values, 512-byte help text</span> <span data-label="Refused as"><code>too_long</code></span></div>
      <div class="row"><span>At most 1024 series per sender, oldest kept</span> <span data-label="Refused as"><code>series_cap</code></span></div>
      <div class="row"><span>A name keeps the kind its first sample set</span> <span data-label="Refused as"><code>type_conflict</code></span></div>
    </div>
    <p>
      A refusal is counted in <code>shep_channel_samples_refused_total</code>
      with a <code>reason</code> label, and the dog prints one line per sender
      and reason in its own log, read with <code>shep bleats metrics</code>.
      A metric that never shows up is otherwise silent.
    </p>
    <p>
      A sender's samples go when it stops, exits, restarts, is deleted or
      errors. A reload frees the drained instance's through its own
      deregistration, never the numbers of the instance now serving.
    </p>

    <h3 id="otlp-export">OTLP export</h3>
    <p>
      The same series can be pushed to an OTLP/HTTP collector instead of
      waited for. <code>otlp_endpoint</code> is the only key that turns it
      on:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="dogs.toml">{`[metrics]
otlp_endpoint = "http://127.0.0.1:4318"
otlp_interval = "60s"
otlp_timeout = "10s"

[metrics.otlp_headers]
Authorization = "Bearer token-from-a-secret-manager"`}</CodeBlock>
    <div class="metrics-table">
      <div class="row header">
        <span>Key</span>
        <span>Meaning</span>
      </div>
      <div class="row"><span><code>otlp_endpoint</code></span> <span data-label="Meaning">Base URL of the collector, <code>http://</code> or <code>https://</code>. Absent means no export, and <code>/v1/metrics</code> is appended to it.</span></div>
      <div class="row"><span><code>otlp_interval</code></span> <span data-label="Meaning">Between exports, <code>60s</code> by default. The first lands one interval after the dog starts.</span></div>
      <div class="row"><span><code>otlp_timeout</code></span> <span data-label="Meaning">One export's budget, connect through response, <code>10s</code> by default.</span></div>
      <div class="row"><span><code>otlp_headers</code></span> <span data-label="Meaning">Extra headers, for a collector that needs auth. Empty by default, and never printed.</span></div>
    </div>
    <p>
      Export is a cargo feature: on in every prebuilt release binary and
      the <code>.deb</code>, off in <code>cargo install shep</code> and the
      Homebrew formula:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock>{`$ cargo install shep --features otel`}</CodeBlock>
    <p>
      A build without it refuses <code>otlp_endpoint</code> at startup,
      exiting <code>invalid_config</code> and naming the feature, rather
      than running a dog that exports nothing while <code>shep list</code>
      reports it online.
    </p>
    <p>
      The body is OTLP/JSON, your samples included: they land after the
      built-in groups, named as <code>/metrics</code> names them. Every
      series above is a <code>gauge</code> except
      <code>shep_sheep_restart_total</code> and the samples you sent as
      counters, monotonic cumulative <code>sum</code>s whose
      <code>startTimeUnixNano</code> is when their sender started, so a
      restart moves it and a collector reads the reset; the dog's own
      counters keep its start. A sample's help text is its
      <code>description</code>, and one with none
      has none. <code>shep_channel_samples_refused_total</code> goes out as
      an integer sum. Integers are decimal strings, rates and sample values
      are numbers, and every Prometheus label is a string attribute, so both
      paths report the same series with the same labels. <code>NaN</code> and
      <code>+Inf</code> reach <code>/metrics</code> but not the export.
    </p>
    <p>
      One failed export is one line in the dog's log, on the first failure
      after a success and again on recovery, never once per tick. A 2xx
      whose body names a <code>partialSuccess</code> counts as one: the line
      carries its <code>rejectedDataPoints</code> and
      <code>errorMessage</code>, and an empty <code>partialSuccess</code> is
      a clean export. Its body is read for that alone, up to 8 KiB. It never
      touches the scrape server or the dog's exit code, and there is no
      final flush on stop: the next tick sends current values anyway.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      The feature adds no dependency, and its cost is code only. Measured on
      one machine (macOS, Apple Silicon, raw file size, not #292's corrected
      install-size method):
    </p>
    <div class="metrics-table">
      <div class="row header">
        <span>Build</span>
        <span>target/release/shep</span>
      </div>
      <div class="row"><span>default</span> <span data-label="target/release/shep">19,069,728 bytes</span></div>
      <div class="row"><span><code>--features otel</code></span> <span data-label="target/release/shep">19,166,944 bytes</span></div>
      <div class="row"><span>delta</span> <span data-label="target/release/shep">97,216 bytes</span></div>
    </div>

    <h2 id="the-bark-dog">The bark dog</h2>
    <p>
      <code>shep enable bark</code> subscribes to the shepherd's event bus
      and delivers to named webhook sinks:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="dogs.toml">{`[bark.sinks]
oncall = { kind = "discord", url = "https://discord.com/api/webhooks/..." }
audit = { kind = "json", url = "https://example.internal/hook" }

[[bark.rules]]
on = "gave_up"
sinks = ["oncall", "audit"]

[[bark.rules]]
on = "restart_rate"
restarts = 5
within = "2m"
sinks = ["oncall"]`}</CodeBlock>
    <p class="fine">
      A sink URL can't carry credentials.{" "}
      <code>https://user:pass@hooks.example.com/</code> is refused when{" "}
      <code>dogs.toml</code> is read, not when a rule first fires: nothing
      here sends an <code>Authorization</code> header, so the pair could
      only be discarded or sent as part of a hostname. Discord and Slack put
      the secret in the path instead, which is why the whole URL is a
      credential.
    </p>
    <p>
      Leave <code>[[bark.rules]]</code> out and bark doesn't go quiet:
      one rule is built in by default, firing on every configured sink
      whenever a sheep reaches <code>Errored</code>. That's the alert that
      must not be missed, so it needs no opt-in. <code>restart_rate</code>{" "}
      is the opposite: an early warning for a sheep flapping but not yet{" "}
      <code>Errored</code>, and it's opt-in because that's exactly the kind
      of thing that pages someone at 3am for a blip if an operator didn't
      choose the threshold themselves.
    </p>
    <Callout variant="careful">
      Verified directly: enable <code>bark</code> with{" "}
      <code>[bark.sinks]</code> absent entirely and it does not run
      quietly with nowhere to send alerts: the default rule still exists
      with zero sinks to route to, bark logs{" "}
      <code>rule 0 routes to no sink at all</code> on every restart attempt,
      and lands on <code>Errored</code>. A dog needs at least one configured
      sink before <code>shep enable bark</code> is worth running.
    </Callout>
    <p>
      Every rule debounces <strong>per subject</strong>, five minutes by
      default, never globally. A global debounce would mean the second
      sheep to go down during an incident goes silent, which is usually the
      incident's most interesting fact. The bus itself
      (<code>tokio::sync::broadcast</code>) drops what a lagging subscriber
      can't keep up with, so bark also polls the flock's state on a timer
      (30s by default) and evaluates the same rules against that snapshot.
      A dropped frame triggers an immediate poll instead of waiting. The two
      routes share one debounce record per subject, so an{" "}
      <code>Errored</code> seen by both fires once, not twice.
    </p>
    <p>
      To alert on a line a sheep prints, add a <code>log_match</code> rule:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="dogs.toml">{`[[bark.rules]]
on = "log_match"
pattern = "FATAL|panicked at"
stream = "err"
sinks = ["oncall"]`}</CodeBlock>
    <p>
      <code>pattern</code> is a regex, unanchored, and can't be empty.{" "}
      <code>stream</code> is <code>out</code>, <code>err</code> or{" "}
      <code>both</code> (the default). The first matching line per sheep
      per debounce window is the one sent; later ones in the window are
      dropped. The shepherd forwards log lines to bark only while a{" "}
      <code>log_match</code> rule exists, and bark can miss lines when a
      sheep outruns it: the poll can't recover a line. The line goes to
      the sink as written, so it can carry a secret.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      Every fired alert is appended to <code>$SHEP_HOME/barks.jsonl</code>, a
      byte-capped ring (1MiB by default) that evicts the oldest record
      first: the file <code>shep barks</code> reads. It's written through
      an advisory file lock, never truncated in place, because two different
      processes append to it: the bark dog when a rule fires, and the
      shepherd itself when an enabled dog exhausts its own restart budget.
    </p>

    <h2 id="reading-the-alert-history">Reading the alert history</h2>
    <VerbSignature verb="barks" usage="shep barks [--tail N]">
      <p>
        Prints that file, newest last, with <code>--tail</code> to see only
        the last few. It reads <code>barks.jsonl</code> straight off disk and
        never connects to the shepherd, which is the whole point: the history
        is on disk precisely so it outlives the shepherd, and the moment this
        verb exists for is an operator reading it after a crash.
      </p>
    </VerbSignature>
    <CodeBlock>{barksDemo}</CodeBlock>
    <p>
      <code>SINKS</code> names each sink the alert was delivered to, with{" "}
      <code>(failed)</code> appended to any that refused it, so a delivery
      failure is visible in the table you are already reading rather than
      only in <code>--format json</code>. It never carries the sink's own
      error text, which can quote a webhook's HTTP response and would widen
      an already-tight column for something the JSON already has in full. A{" "}
      <code>-</code> means the shepherd wrote the record itself, with no
      sinks and no webhook code behind it, which is the case above.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      A line that won't parse costs you that one record, not the history. A
      writer that died mid-append, or a record from a newer shep, is skipped
      and the rest still prints. This file is read during an incident, and
      refusing the whole ring over one bad line would be the wrong failure
      mode.
    </p>

    <h2 id="writing-your-own">Writing your own</h2>
    <p>
      A dog is an ordinary binary that answers a few questions on stdout and
      speaks the dog protocol on a socket the shepherd hands it. The whole
      contract, including what <code>--version</code> and{" "}
      <code>--schema</code> have to answer and why the binary is the only
      thing that can answer them, is its own chapter:{" "}
      <a href="/docs/writing-a-dog">Writing a dog</a>.
    </p>

    <details>
      <summary><h2 id="what-shep-writes-into-a-dog-s-own-log">What shep writes into a dog's own log</h2></summary>
      <p>
      A dog's log is the first place anyone looks when a dog misbehaves, and
      it used to hold only what the dog itself said. Everything shep had to
      say about it went to <code>shepd.err.log</code>, which is not the file
      shep's own error messages tell you to open. Shep now writes its own
      account into the dog's log too, marked <code>[shep]</code> so it can't
      be mistaken for the dog's output.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock>{dogLogDemo}</CodeBlock>
    <p>
      Six things get written: the spawn and the resolved binary path, an
      accepted handshake (once, the first time this shepherd hears from the
      dog, not on every reconnect), a refused handshake with both
      protocol numbers, the warning when a dog goes quiet, the verdict when
      shep gives up on it, and the exit code or signal when its process
      stops. <code>shep bleats &lt;name&gt; --follow</code> sees the same
      lines live, marked the same way, interleaved with the dog's own output
      in arrival order.
    </p>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary><h2 id="when-a-dog-stops-answering">When a dog stops answering</h2></summary>
      <p>
      A dog that is running but never handshakes gets one restart from disk
      and then a verdict. That verdict says what the shepherd actually
      watched happen, because the three cases it reports have opposite
      fixes.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Nothing has ever connected from the dog's process.</strong>{" "}
        The dog isn't reaching the socket. Rebuild or reinstall it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>
          Its process has connected, and never named a dog in its handshake.
        </strong>{" "}
        The dog is talking to shep and may be serving every request it's
        asked; what it doesn't do is say who it is. Reinstalling the same
        build won't change that: it's built against shep-client older
        than 0.1.23, or it calls <code>Client::connect</code> where it should
        call <code>ReconnectingClient::connect_as_dog</code>. Rebuild it
        against a current shep-client.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Shep couldn't tell which process opened the connections.</strong>{" "}
        Windows has no peer identity by design, and some unix platforms
        decline to report a pid. Neither of the two above is ruled out, so
        shep names both and tells you to read the dog's own log: a dog that
        can't reach the socket says so there, and one that's merely
        anonymous doesn't.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="fine">
      Every one of them ends with a command to run. The one before them said{" "}
      <em>the binary on disk cannot talk to this shep either</em> in all
      three cases, which sent one operator through two days of reinstalling a
      dog that was connected and serving requests the whole time.
    </p>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary><h2 id="when-a-dog-dies">When a dog dies</h2></summary>
      <p>
      If an enabled dog exhausts its own restart budget and lands on{" "}
      <code>Errored</code>, the shepherd writes a record straight into{" "}
      <code>barks.jsonl</code> itself, independent of whether the bark dog is
      even running. That matters for one reason: bark has no webhook code of
      its own for reporting its own death, so if bark is the one that died,
      nothing pages anyone about it over Discord or Slack. What you get
      instead is a local record (<code>shep barks</code> after the fact)
      and the metrics dog's <code>shep_dog_up</code> gauge dropping to{" "}
      <code>0</code> for that dog's name.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      Nothing watches across dogs, and that's not an oversight: the
      supervisor that restarts a crashed dog has no idea what a "dog" is
      beyond the marker on its entry, so no dog is positioned to watch
      another dog the way the shepherd watches all of them.{" "}
      <code>shep barks</code> and the metrics endpoint are the two places
      that say a dog went down.
    </p>
    </details>
</article>
